## Additional Detailed Description Language

In certain embodiments, the azimuth actuation assembly rotates the support platform, the antenna assembly, or both through a continuous or limited angular range about a generally vertical axis. The leveling actuation assembly may include one or more independently controllable linear or rotary actuators arranged to compensate for inclination of the base. The elevation actuation assembly changes the boresight elevation of the selected antenna without requiring a corresponding change in the leveled orientation of the support platform.

The controller may receive target coordinates, pointing vectors, orbital data, geographic coordinates, platform-attitude data, or direct angular commands. Based on such information, the controller may calculate desired azimuth and elevation values and may compensate those values for measured roll, pitch, yaw, installation offsets, actuator backlash, structural deflection, or sensor calibration parameters.

The primary and secondary antennas may be mounted to a common movable structure or to respective movable structures. Selection may be performed electrically by an RF switching network, logically by controlling respective transceiver channels, mechanically by repositioning a selected antenna, or by a combination thereof. The secondary antenna may provide redundancy, an alternative operating band, a different polarization, a wider acquisition beam, or a different gain characteristic.

Closed-loop control may be performed independently for each actuation axis or through a coordinated multivariable control process. Feedback may include absolute or incremental position measurements, motor-current measurements, velocity measurements, inertial measurements, satellite-navigation data, received-signal metrics, beacon-tracking information, or combinations thereof. The controller may continuously or periodically update actuator commands until each measured position is within a corresponding tolerance of its commanded position.
